[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# Solitaire Design Lessons\n\n## Search Space and Control\n- Treat redeals as a major complexity multiplier. Unlimited redeals can turn otherwise reasonable games into cyclic search problems, especially if winning also requires emptying the stock or waste.\n- When a solver times out by revisiting too many states, first reduce repeatable access to the same cards (such as redeals or equivalent move paths) before loosening the game's core constraints.\n- Restricting moves to pile tops is a strong default for face-up tableau games; allowing extraction from deep inside piles often creates many equivalent branches without adding meaningful decisions.\n\n## Movement Rules and Mobility\n- In fully visible layouts, movement rules should create real tactical mobility, not just legal micro-moves. If single-card shuffling leaves many cards \"almost usable\" but hard to deploy, a constrained multi-card move can improve both solvability and decision quality.\n- Make transport rules looser than completion rules when the goal is to assemble strict runs, sets, or ordered sequences. If movement demands the same pattern as final completion, the game often stalls before its main puzzle appears.\n- Prefer narrowly constrained flexibility over unrestricted freedom: well-bounded stack moves can open play, while broad movement from anywhere in a pile usually only adds search noise.\n\n## Objectives and Rule Interaction\n- Check win conditions for harmful interactions with recycling rules. Objectives that require fully exhausting the stock become much harder and more repetitive when players can loop through the same cards indefinitely.\n- Build rules and objective rules should complement each other rather than duplicate each other's strictness. Let the challenge come from planning toward the goal, not from blocking all intermediate reorganization.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 4 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a tableau game repeatedly times out rather than exhausting, the problem is often insufficient mobility inside a large state space, not simply low raw win rate. A small relaxation on empty-pile fills can create much more productive reorganization than adding redeals or broad new move types.\n2. In open-information tableau games, holding cells or reserves should usually connect to the completion path, not just to transport. If temporary storage can\u2019t feed the goal directly, the puzzle often becomes search-heavy because the player must \"undo\" storage through extra tableau moves before any real progress can be scored.\n3. When a fully visible tableau game loses mostly by search explosion rather than immediate exhaustion, the problem is often insufficient reorganization rather than insufficient card access. Adding a narrow transport move between tableau piles can improve solvability and decision quality much more cleanly than loosening the objective or draw structure.\n4. When a solitaire's movement rule requires the same structure as its scoring/completion rule, the game often stalls before players can assemble anything meaningful. A good fix is to loosen transport rules while keeping completion strict, so mobility supports the real objective instead of duplicating its difficulty.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# Solitaire Design Lessons\n\n## Search Space and Control\n- Treat redeals as a major complexity multiplier. Unlimited or easy recycling can turn otherwise reasonable games into cyclic search problems, especially if winning also requires exhausting the stock or waste.\n- When a solver times out by revisiting too many states, first reduce repeatable access loops and redundant move paths before loosening the game's core constraints.\n- Restricting moves to pile tops is a strong default for face-up tableau games; allowing extraction from deep inside piles often creates many equivalent branches without adding meaningful decisions.\n\n## Movement Rules and Mobility\n- In fully visible layouts, timeout-heavy losses usually indicate weak internal mobility more than weak card access. Improve reorganization inside the tableau before adding redeals or other broad access tools.\n- Prefer small, targeted mobility boosts over global freedom. Relaxing empty-pile fill rules or adding a narrow tableau-to-tableau transport move often improves solvability more cleanly than broad new move types.\n- Make transport rules looser than completion rules when the goal is to assemble strict runs, sets, or ordered sequences. If movement demands the same pattern as final completion, the game often stalls before its main puzzle appears.\n- Prefer narrowly constrained flexibility over unrestricted freedom: bounded stack moves and limited transport can open play, while broad movement from anywhere in a pile usually only adds search noise.\n\n## Objectives and Supporting Structures\n- Check win conditions for harmful interactions with recycling rules. Objectives that require fully exhausting the stock become much harder and more repetitive when players can loop through the same cards indefinitely.\n- Build rules and objective rules should complement each other rather than duplicate each other's strictness. Let the challenge come from planning toward the goal, not from blocking all intermediate reorganization.\n- Holding cells, reserves, or temporary storage should usually connect directly to the completion path, not only to transport. If stored cards must be reintroduced through extra tableau moves before they can score, the game tends to become search-heavy and inefficient.\n```"}]